L354: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L355: **1.** **INTRODUCTION**
L356: 1.1 BACKGROUND
L357: Exercises, specifically resistance and aerobic training, have been demonstrated to improve cognitive
L358: outcomes, along with improved physical capacity and mobility[1,2]. Both aerobic [3] and resistance
L359: training[4] trials of different durations have revealed impressive results, with the most consistent
L360: findings being observed after combined interventions, multimodal exercise- lasting roughly six months
L361: to one year[1]. For example, our group has recently reported that aerobic exercise improves performance
L362: on learning a new sequence of movements. Although the training benefits of progressive resistance
L363: training (PRT) have been well documented, PRT has been studied far less extensively than aerobic
L364: training in patients with Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI).
L365: Exercise training is beneficial for cognition even in frail older adults and in those with low mobility as
L366: has been shown by our group[4]. Exact mechanisms supporting the benefits of exercises for cognition
L367: in humans need to be further explored but numerous studies in animals and humans have demonstrated
L368: that aerobic exercise may have neuroprotective and neurorestorative effects [4,5,5,6]. The rationale of
L369: combining aerobic and progressive resistance training as a multimodal exercise intervention is supported
L370: by research that has revealed potential beneficial effects on insulin-like growth factor-1, insulin
L371: sensitivity, and anti-inflammatory and brain-derived neurotrophic factor pathways, which are related to
L372: both sarcopenia and cognitive decline[7]. In addition, multimodal exercise interventions have shown
L373: positive effects on muscle/lean mass, cognition and brain volume [8].
L374: Similarly, cognitive training (e.g. computer based cognitive process training) has also shown positive
L375: results in improving cognition, mobility and postural control. Several recent systematic reviews on the
L376: topic[9,10] support the cognitive benefits of training and a seated dual-task cognitive training regimen
L377: designed by our group has demonstrated that this type of training can also improve balance in healthy
L378: older adults.[11] In line with exercise training, recent research on cognitive training has also supported
L379: important improvements in brain plasticity post-intervention [12,13].
L380: In addition, vitamin D deficiency has been linked to cognitive dysfunction, dementia and mobility
L381: decline. [1,14-16] Besides its very well-known effects on muscle and bone physiology, vitamin D is
L382: considered a neurosteroid hormone which exhibits neuroprotective attributes through antioxidative
L383: mechanisms, neuronal calcium regulation, immunomodulation, enhanced nerve conduction and
L384: detoxification mechanisms.[14,17-20] Compelling evidence from animal models and epidemiological
L385: studies supports a potential beneficial role for vitamin D on cognitive function. In this trial, we will test
L386: 6
L387: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L388: if vitamin D supplementation will have a synergistic effect in improving or stabilizing cognitive function
L389: in combination with exercises and cognitive training.
L390: Mild Cognitive Impairment is thought to be the optimum stage to intervene with preventive strategies
L391: and early treatments, in fact it is estimated that one-year delay in dementia incidence could save $109
L392: billion over a 30 years period the Canadian Health System.[21,22] Robustly designed trials, with
L393: longitudinal follow-up, have been recommended in MCI to investigate the comparative benefits of
L394: isolated and combined physical exercises and cognitive training. To date, the effect of adding cognitive
L395: training and vitamin D to multimodal physical exercises for improving global cognition, executive
L396: function and memory in MCI has not been assessed.[23]
L397: The proposed **SYNERGIC TRIAL (SYN** chronizing **E** xercises, and **R** emedies in **G** a **I** t and **C** ognition)
L398: is uniquely designed to evaluate the effect of aerobic and progressive resistance training exercises,
L399: isolated or combined with cognitive training and vitamin D supplementation, in older adults with MCI.
L400: This trial will be conducted by the Motor Exercise and Cognition Team which is part of the Canadian
L401: Consortium in Neurodegeneration and Aging (CCNA) and the Canadian Gait and Cognition Network
L402: (CGCN).
L403: **2.** **STUDY DESIGN AND HYPOTHESES**
L404: 2.1. HYPOTHESES
L405: **2.1.a Primary Hypotheses**
L406: 1. Twenty weeks (five months) of supervised multimodal exercise (aerobic and progressive
L407: resistance training) alone and/or in combination with cognitive training (CT) and/or oral
L408: vitamin D will significantly improve global cognitive function in older adults with MCI, as
L409: assessed by primary outcome **Alzheimer’s Disease Assessment Scale-Cognitive subscale**
L410: **plus (ADAS-Cog Plus).**
L411: 2. Multimodal exercise alone and/or in combination with CT and/or oral vitamin D would
L412: significantly improve secondary cognitive outcomes across memory, executive function, and
L413: attention, as well mobility outcomes related to falls risk and dual-task gait, and dual-task gait
L414: cost.
L415: 7
L416: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L417: 3. The combination of CT and oral vitamin D with multimodal exercise will be significantly
L418: superior to either intervention alone for cognitive and functional benefits, demonstrating a
L419: synergistic effect.
L420: 4. Mechanistically, our intervention will significantly improve hippocampi volume, blood
L421: biomarkers, and BDNF levels [24], and fMRI prefrontal activation in executive control dual
L422: task condition.
L423: **2.1.b Secondary Hypotheses**
L424: 1. All active training interventions will improve brain morphology and biochemistry compared with
L425: control –placebo-interventions as defined by: increased hippocampal volume (mm [3] ) by MRI
L426: scanning; positive localized Voxel-Based Morphometry (VBM) brain changes (z-score relative
L427: change); decreased whole brain volume of White Matter Hyper-intensities (WMHs) (mm [3] ); and
L428: lead to beneficial hippocampal and anterior and posterior cingulate MRS metabolite changes (%
L429: increase in N-acetylaspartate, and increase in phosphocreatine metabolites).
L430: 2. All active interventions will improve secondary cognitive outcomes, in the domains of attention,
L431: memory, fluency, and executive function, relative to the control condition, and combined
L432: training will be superior to either single intervention.
L433: 3. Multimodal exercise will preferentially decrease inflammatory markers, and increase fitness
L434: (strength and aerobic capacity), muscle strength, and functional mobility, compared to either
L435: cognitive or control condition.
L436: 4. Cognitive and physical training will produce positive effects on psychological health and quality
L437: of life (SF-36) above and beyond the non-specific effects seen after control condition.
L438: **3.** **DESIGN**
L439: The **SYNERGIC TRIAL (SYN** chronizing **E** xercises, and **R** emedies in **G** a **I** t and **C** ognition) is a
L440: Randomized, modified factorial design with parallel-groups (five arms), and double-blind study
L441: **evaluating the cognitive effect of multimodal exercises (as a main intervention) combined with and**
L442: **without cognitive training and vitamin D** . This is achieved using 4 arms (1 to 4) and a 5 [th] arm is added
L443: as a control group. Figure 1 illustrates the trial design and Figure 2 summarizes the timeline of the trial
L444: consisting of an approximate 12-18 months enrolment period, and 12 months of follow-up. The trial
L445: adheres to the Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials (CONSORT) guidelines for conducting and
L446: reporting clinical trials, as extended to non-pharmacologic interventions. Two hundred (200)
L447: 8
L448: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L449: participants with Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI), aged 60 and older will be enrolled and randomized
L450: into one of five arms.
L451: Arm 1: **multimodal exercises + cognitive training + Vitamin D**
L452: Arm 2: **multimodal exercises + cognitive training** + placebo D
L453: Arm 3: **multimodal exercises** + control cognitive training + **Vitamin D**
L454: Arm 4: **multimodal exercises** + control cognitive training+ placebo D;
L455: Arm 5: tone-stretching exercises + control cognitive training+ placebo D.
L456: Note: in **bold** are the active interventions. Arm 5 is the arm with pure control interventions.
L457: Figure 1 shows the flow chart of the trial. **E** : multimodal exercises; **CT:** cognitive training; **cE** : control
L458: tone-stretching exercises; and **cCT:** control cognitive training.
L459: **4. STUDY OUTCOMES**
L460: 4.1 PRIMARY OUTCOME: Global cognition assessed using the ADAS-Cog Plus.[25]
L461: We expect that our combined interventions, over 20 weeks, will show improvement and/or less decline
L462: in **global** **cognition.** We expect a three (3) point change (continuous variable) across full intervention/
L463: control groups on the ADAS-Cog Plus scores, a value we considered clinically significant based in
L464: previous studies using the ADAS-Cog 13 items. This expected change/ improvement is similar in
L465: magnitude to the improvements seen in positive pharmacological clinical trials in dementia in the
L466: ADAS-Cog .[26] Additionally, improving in the proportion of participants, expressed as percentages per
L467: allocated groups, with abnormal ADAS-Cog Plus (dichotomous variable) after intervention will be
L468: considered success of the intervention. ADAS-Cog Plus is described in detail in Section 7. We also
L469: expect a significant statistical improvement in the overall transformed score of ADAS-Cog Plus
L470: calculated using a validated scoring system[27] Observing a statistically significant difference in this
L471: primary outcome will be considered preliminary evidence of efficacy.
L472: 4.2 SECONDARY OUTCOMES
L473: Secondary outcomes include neuroimaging, cognitive (neuropsychological assessments), gait and
L474: mobility outcomes including incidence of fall, blood biomarkers, quality of life scorings, as described in
L475: our primary and secondary hypotheses. Secondary outcomes are described in detail in Section 7.
L476: 9
L477: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L478: **5. STUDY POPULATION**
L479: 5.1 PARTICIPANTS, RECRUITMENT, STRATIFICATION, AND BLINDING
L480: We will target older adults aged 60 and over with MCI. Interventions will be done across five sites:
L481: London (lead and sponsor site, target recruitment: 70 participants), Waterloo-University of Waterloo
L482: (target recruitment: 25), Waterloo-Wilfrid Laurier University (target recruitment: 20), Montreal (target
L483: recruitment: 30) and Vancouver (target recruitment: 55). Each intervention site may have more than one
L484: recruitment center. For those who are interested in participating, a consent and screening session will be
L485: arranged as described in our participant flowchart, Figure 3.
L486: Once informed consent is obtained, we will perform the screening tests. Those who remain eligible after
L487: the screening session including the Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire Plus (PARQ+) and who
L488: provide written recommendation from their physician or site physician indicating their appropriateness
L489: to participate in an aerobic-based exercise training program will proceed to baseline assessments.
L490: Participants will be informed that they will be randomly assigned to one of five arms by the research
L491: coordinator and will be blinded to the investigators' hypothesis as to which is the preferred intervention
L492: arm.
L493: All groups will have an equal volume and frequency of contact with trainers over the 12 months of the
L494: study. All primary and secondary outcomes will be obtained and analyzed by blinded assessors on
L495: different days to the training programs.
L496: 5.2 INCLUSION AND EXCLUSION CRITERIA
L497: _Inclusion criteria:_
L498: Participants must meet each of the following criteria for enrolment into the study:
L499: 1. At least 60 years of age.
L500: 2. Self-reported levels of proficiency in English or French (at Montreal site only) for speaking
L501: and understanding spoken language.
L502: 3. Able to comply with scheduled visits, treatment plan, and other trial procedures.
L503: 4. Able to ambulate at least 10 m independently.
L504: 5. Having MCI defined following Albert et al.[28] criteria as:
L505: a-Objective cognitive impairment in one of the following four cognitive domains: memory,
L506: executive function, attention, and language operationalized using one or more of the
L507: following: MoCA Test (Montreal Cognitive Assessment) with scores ranging from 13-24/30,
L508: Logical Memory below ADNI cutoffs, CERAD word list recall < 6.
L509: 10
L510: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L511: b-Preserved activities of daily living operationalized as a Score >14/23 on the Lawton-Brody
L512: IADL[29] scale and confirmed by clinician’s interviews.
L513: c-Absence of dementia using criteria from the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental
L514: Disorders, Fifth Edition [30] and/or Global CDR = 0.5.
L515: 6. Having normal or corrected to normal vision in at least one eye so that they can identify
L516: symbols and stimuli presented on a computer screen in front of them.
L517: 7. Must be in sufficient health to participate in the study’s aerobic-based exercise training
L518: program. This will be determined using the PARQ+ screening tool and with an evaluation by
L519: a certified exercise physiologist and/or a physician, when appropriate indicating clearance to
L520: participate in an aerobic-based exercise training program.
L521: _Exclusion criteria:_
L522: Participants who exhibit any of the following conditions are to be excluded from the study:
L523: 1. Serious underlying disease which, in the opinion of the investigator, may preclude
L524: engagement in interventions or may interfere with the participant’s ability to participate fully
L525: in the study.
L526: 2. Participant with uncontrolled major depression, schizophrenia, severe anxiety and drug
L527: abuse.
L528: 3. Current parkinsonism or any neurological disorder with residual motor deficits (e.g. stroke
L529: with motor deficit), active musculoskeletal disorders (e.g. severe osteoarthritis of lower
L530: limbs) or history of knee/hip replacement affecting gait performance at clinical evaluation
L531: 4. Intention to enroll in other clinical trials during the same time period.
L532: 5. Pre-existing or current active participation in physical activity program involving aerobic or
L533: resistance training regimen in previous 6 months.
L534: 6. Taking Cognitive enhancers, Neuroleptics, Anticholinergics, or Vitamin D in doses more
L535: than 1000IU/day or equivalent.
L536: 7. Known hypercalcemia and/or disorder that is cause of hypercalcemia (e.g.
L537: hyperparathyroidism/ Paget’s disease/ Sarcoidosis).
L538: 8. Known renal/kidney insufficiency.
L539: 9. Uncontrolled hypertension.
L540: 10. Uncontrolled diabetes.
L541: 11
L542: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L543: 5.3 SAMPLE SIZE:
L544: Sample size calculation is based on changes in our main primary outcome: stabilization/improvement in
L545: cognition as measured using the **Alzheimer Disease Assessment Scale Cognitive Plus (** ADAS-Cog
L546: Plus **)** . We expect a change of three (3) points on the ADAS-Cog Plus scores, a value that is considered
L547: clinically significant, and it is similar in magnitude to the improvements seen in positive
L548: pharmacological clinical trials in dementia.[26] Two previous studies testing the effect of exercises on
L549: cognition in 200 MCI participants shown a mean score of 6-7 and a standard deviation of 3 in the
L550: ADAS-Cog.[24,31] Based on these data and assuming a mean change of 3 points in the ADAS-Cog, 34
L551: participants per arm are required to have 80% power to detect a change of three points or more on the
L552: ADAS-Cog with a 2-tailed α error of 5%, adjusted for 10 potential pairwise comparisons. We estimate a
L553: worst case scenario of 15% of dropout rate during the 20 weeks of follow up to the main endpoint.
L554: Therefore, we will aim to recruit 40 per arm for a total of 200 participants.
L555: Sample size estimates per group for the primary outcome: change in ADAS-Cog Plus
L556: Reference: Independent Groups - Machin D, Campbell M, Fayers, P, Pinol A (1997) Sample Size Tables for Clinical Studies
L557: Second Ed. Blackwell Science IBSN 0-86542-870-0 p. 24-25
L558: **6. STUDY PROCESS**
L559: 6.1 INTERVENTIONS
L560: All participants will complete three (3) group training sessions per week (total 20 weeks), under the
L561: supervision of trainers. Each session will last approximately 90 minutes and will be comprised of 30
L562: minutes cognitive training (CT) or control cognitive training (cCT) followed by approximately 60
L563: minutes of combined aerobic and progressive strengthening exercise (E) or control tone-stretching
L564: exercise (cE) and. All participants will receive a vitamin D (tablet 10,000 IU) or placebo three times per
L565: week.
L566: Within each small group of up to eight individuals, participants follow the program tailored to their
L567: individual functioning level, with constant monitoring by the trainers. Participants are expected to
L568: attend all training sessions and research staff will strongly encourage them to do so. Each training group
L569: will have 1-2 trainers present each session to ensure a ratio of one (1) trainer per four (4) participants. To
L570: 12
L571: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L572: avoid potential imbalances in exposure time, control interventions for exercise and cognitive training
L573: will have the same duration.
L574: Throughout the 12 month trial, participants will be provided with falls calendars, in which they will
L575: record any falls that have occurred, and they will be asked to bring them at the training sessions. At the
L576: completion of the 20-week intervention, participants are not given ongoing access to the training. During
L577: follow-up, participants will be called three months after their completion of the intervention to track
L578: incidence of falls, adverse outcomes, such as emergency room visits and change of medications;
L579: physical activity will be also be tracked using the Physical Activity Scale for the Elderly (PASE).
L580: Following assessments at 6 and 12 months, participants will receive a compensation of $50 for their
L581: participation for a total of $100.
L582: 6.1.1 COGNITIVE TRAINING (MULTIMODAL CT-INTERVENTION)
L583: Cognitive Training (CT) intervention will involve tablet-based multimodal and multi-domain dual-task
L584: training with memory load. The training uses the custom-written program, developed for neuro
L585: rehabilitation and used in previous research trials for cognitive [32-34] and mobility outcomes [11].
L586: Training sessions will take place in groups of up to eight participants before each of the fitness-training
L587: session for duration of 30 min max. Participants will perform a concurrent visuo-motor task (dual-task
L588: combination) composed of different sets of visual stimuli that have to be identified by tapping
L589: designated figures on an iPad (or an Android tablet). Participants will perform discrimination tasks
L590: involving sets of items (e.g. letters, numbers, animals, vehicles, fruits, celestial bodies). Each session
L591: will involve two sets of items, one for each task. The combination of sets of items will change every
L592: four sessions.
L593: Task instructions will always be administered by a trained Research Assistant. Within each training
L594: session and for each task combination, participants will complete a first block of single-pure (SP) trials
L595: followed by mixed-trial blocks with single-task trials (SM) intermixed and dual-mixed trials (DM: 2
L596: tasks at once). Each training session will involve 80 SP, 128 SM and 192 DM trials (thus 40% of the
L597: DM blocks will be SM trials). Within the DM trials, the first 30 training sessions will be performed
L598: following a fixed priority instruction (participants will be asked to keep an equal priority on left and
L599: right hands.) The following 30 sessions will be performed following a variable priority instruction
L600: (participants will be told to vary priority from one hand to another in different blocks). Participants will
L601: complete a total of 60 sessions. Importantly, an adaptive continuous feedback on performance was will
L602: be provided during the dual-mixed block of the training sessions.
L603: 13
L604: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L605: Feedback will take the shape of a speedometer whose indicator moves and changes colour (green,
L606: yellow, orange or red) to inform participants of their response speed. Two speedometers (one for each
L607: hand) will be displayed at the top of the screen. Each speedometer is associated to one task. In equal
L608: priority blocks, participants will be asked to try to maintain both speedometers at least in the yellow
L609: zone and prevent them from turning to red. In variable priority blocks, participants will be asked to
L610: maintain the speedometer associated to the prioritized task in the green zone, while the other
L611: speedometer can be at least in the orange zone. The colour of the speedometers will be determined by
L612: the average reaction time (RT) on the last three trials’ RT for the DM block compared to the median RT
L613: for the SM trials multiplied by a factor of 1.5 (for the first training session, as there are no previous SM
L614: trials, the DM block will be compared to 1500ms). In addition, a feedback will be provided at the end of
L615: the session, where participants will be informed of mean RT and accuracy achieved throughout sessions
L616: (presented in a histogram without explicit values).
L617: 6.1.2 EXERCISES (MULTIMODAL EXERCISE – INTERVENTION)
L618: The exercises training program involves 3 weekly sessions of approximately 60 min each. All sessions
L619: will be held in appropriate gym facilities and take place between Monday and Friday ensuring that there
L620: are not three consecutive days (i.e. training can occur on Monday, Wednesday and Friday or Monday,
L621: Thursday and Friday etc.). Staff with experience and/or certification in exercises (graduate student in
L622: kinesiology, Canadian Society of Exercise Physiology certificates, etc.) will supervise all training
L623: sessions with at least one coach per four participants.
L624: After a general warm-up, participants will execute the strength-training portion by performing light
L625: strengthening exercises (pushes and pulls using elastic bands, chair stands, and forward lunges). The
L626: next phase will include the following exercises: leg press, leg flexion (lower body), seated press, seated
L627: row, and seated pull (upper body). These exercises are made in a circuit alternating between lower and
L628: upper body exercises. A list of suggested exercises base on muscle groups will be provided to the
L629: trainers (see appendix II). The recovery period between exercises corresponds to the time needed to
L630: move from one station to another. While the first portion of this training protocol (12 weeks) will be
L631: dedicated to strength endurance exercises the following 12 weeks will focus on maximal strength.
L632: Training volume and intensity are described in the table below. Sets represent the number of circuit
L633: rounds while repetitions are maximal, which means that the resistance must be adjusted by the trainer so
L634: that the participant reaches exhaustion at the last prescribed repetition of each set. Training prescription
L635: for all exercises is made in accordance to the American College of Sport Medicine (ACSM) guidelines
L636: for strength development in older adults (ACSM, version 1998).
L637: 14
L638: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
