L661: Overall rate of perceived exertion (RPE) will be monitored using a Borg scale (0-10) 30 minutes after
L662: the end of each session. Participants will have to rate the overall difficulty of the training session. These
L663: results will provide information about the training load (session RPE – sRPE), monotony and strain.
L664: 6.1.3 VITAMIN D SUPPLEMENTATION
L665: Participants will receive Vitamin D supplementation (1 tablet of 10,000IU of Vitamin D3) or matching
L666: placebo three times per week in order to reach a weekly cumulative dose of 30,000 IU per week
L667: (equivalent of 4,258 IU daily). The vitamin D capsules will be provided by the Research Coordinator
L668: 15
L669: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L670: (RC) in vials at baseline assessment and every four weeks to complete 5 vials during the training period.
L671: Vials will be returned by the participants to the RC at the end of each 4 weeks block. Rationale and bio
L672: safety of the dose: The dose of 10,000 IU/day is currently approved by Health Canada as a
L673: supplementation for elderly patients. Heaney et al. have administered doses of 30,000 IU/day Vitamin
L674: D3
L675: to adult men for five months, with no significant changes in serum calcium concentrations or adverse
L676: reactions.[35-37] A comprehensive review of toxic effects of vitamin D found that the lowest level at
L677: which an adverse effect was observed was a serum calcidiol concentration of 200 nmol/l, corresponding
L678: to a daily intake of 40,000 IU. Therefore, three weekly doses of 10,000IU have a 9-fold weekly margin
L679: of safety of the established safe dose.
L680: 6.2 CONTROL/PLACEBOS
L681: **6.2.1 Control Cognitive Training**
L682: The active control cognitive training group will participate in skills training courses based on a
L683: published study from our research group[38]. Each session will consist of Internet search for 3 hotels, 3
L684: touristic places and 3 restaurants, of participant’s preference, in cities determined by the instructor at the
L685: beginning of each training session (a list of cities is presented in the manual of procedures for cognitive
L686: training). Another task will be watching a 20 minutes National Geographic documentary movie selected
L687: by the instructor at the beginning of each session; at the end of the movie participants need to answer
L688: three questions about the watched movie. These iPad tasks will be alternated between sessions to
L689: prevent boredom. The approximate time to perform both tasks is 20 minutes which is the approximate
L690: time to complete the dual-task training on the iPad.
L691: Active control sessions will be held in groups of up to 8 participants and will take place in the same
L692: context and physical environment used for dual-task training sessions and lead by the same instructors.
L693: Studies compared dual-task training to single-task training, as a control condition, in order to control for
L694: exposure of the same specific tasks performed separately [38]. However, it can be argued that single
L695: task training is not engaging or stimulating enough and does not provide a feeling of progression. In
L696: response to this, Walton et al. [39] recommended that cognitive training studies use preferably sham
L697: trainings whereby clinician interaction and participant expectation effects can be reliably matched.
L698: Therefore, this study will compare dual-task training to a non-specific intervention, more comparable to
L699: cognitively stimulating activities that older adults can naturally engage in. To assure same time exposure
L700: and same social interaction than in active intervention, the control intervention will be held in groups
L701: using the same type of device that is used for the active intervention.
L702: 16
L703: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L704: 6.2.2 Placebo Vitamin D supplementation
L705: Participants will receive Vitamin D supplementation (1 tablet of 10,000IU of Vitamin D3) or matching
L706: placebo three times per week in order to reach a weekly cumulative dose of 30,000 IU per week
L707: (equivalent of 4,258 IU daily). The vitamin D capsules will be provided by the Research Coordinator
L708: (RC) in vials at baseline assessment and every four weeks to complete 5 vials during the training period.
L709: Vials will be returned by the participants to the RC at the end of each 4 weeks block. Rationale and bio
L710: safety of the dose: The dose of 10,000 IU/day is currently approved by Health Canada as a
L711: supplementation for elderly patients. Heaney et al. have administered doses of 30,000 IU/day Vitamin
L712: D3
L713: to adult men for five months, with no significant changes in serum calcium concentrations or adverse
L714: reactions.[35-37] A comprehensive review of toxic effects of vitamin D found that the lowest level at
L715: which an adverse effect was observed was a serum calcidiol concentration of 200 nmol/l, corresponding
L716: to a daily intake of 40,000 IU. Therefore, three weekly doses of 10,000IU have a 9-fold weekly margin
L717: of safety of the established safe dose.
L718: **6.2.3 Control Exercises (Tone-Stretching Exercises)**
L719: Participants assigned in the control exercise condition will take part in tone-stretching sessions in groups
L720: of up to 8 participants, supervised by a trainer. The exercises will be devoted to improve muscle tone,
L721: flexibility, without improving, strength, cardiorespiratory capacity (and mobility). Resistant load and
L722: number of repetitions will not progress across exercise sessions. The session will start with a 5-minute
L723: warm-up on the machine of their choice. This will be followed by 50 minutes of stretching exercises that
L724: will target the entire body, one joint at a time, from head to toes. Exercises will be performed in a seated
L725: position as much as possible. The stretching program will be based on the one developed by Stanziano et
L726: al.[39] The session will end with 5 minutes of relaxation. Variability in exercises performed between
L727: sessions will be encouraged in order to avoid progression throughout the entire program.
L728: **6.3 Supervision**
L729: All physical exercise training will be fully supervised by research assistants trained in exercise
L730: physiology or with kinesiology or physical therapy backgrounds or training. Training frequency is 3
L731: days/week. Each session will take 90 minutes on average.
L732: **7.0 STUDY PROCEDURES**
L733: Baseline measurements will be obtained prior to randomization. There will be three measurement
L734: sessions: baseline, 6 months, and 12 months (Figure 1, Appendix I). Outcomes will be assessed by
L735: 17
L736: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L737: trained assessors blinded to group allocation. As outlined in point 5, there are five main
L738: interventional/assessment centers. Recruitment will be occurring primary in clinics serving MCI
L739: populations already identifies in each site and from the community. In addition, older persons belonging
L740: to the community will be invited to participate through different recruitment methods, including case
L741: findings by phone interviews and recruitments in primary care and geriatrics clinics. Strategies to
L742: increase the awareness of the study will be implemented through explanatory brochures and a dedicated
L743: webpage: “www.synergictrial.com”. Results of the mobility tests and laboratory examinations can be
L744: provided to the participant and to her/his family physician upon their request. Transportation will be
L745: paid when necessary. Finally, dissemination of our findings will be conducted in the community through
L746: open discussion sessions and booklets and publications in the local newsletter.
L747: 7.1 SCREENING SESSION (VISIT 1)
L748: The following study procedures will be performed:
L749: 1. Written informed consent from study participant
L750: 2. Demographic Information
L751: 3. Physical Activity Scale for the Elderly Questionnaire (PASE)
L752: 4. he Mini Mental State Examination (MMSE)
L753: 5. The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA), Logical Memory 1 & 2, Consortium to Establish a
L754: Registry for Alzheimer’s Disease (CERAD) Word List Recall
L755: 6. Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS-30)
L756: 7. Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7 (GAD-7)
L757: 8. Clinical Dementia Rating Scale (CDR)
L758: 9. Alzheimer's Disease Cooperative Study Activities of Daily Living (ADCS-ADL) and Lawton
L759: Brody Instrumental Activities Of Daily Living (IADL) Scale
L760: 10. Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire Plus (PAR-Q+)
L761: 11. Physical Activity Tracker allocated to study participant
L762: During the screening session, global cognitive function will be assessed using the MMSE and the
L763: MoCA[40,41] and Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire Plus (PAR-Q+) will be also done.
L764: Physical activity trackers (ActiGraph) will be provided to each participant during the screening session.
L765: The physical activity tracker will be worn by participants until first baseline assessment visit, which is
L766: scheduled up to four weeks after screening. This strategy will assure an accurate record of physical
L767: activity for each participant prior to participating in the training protocol and will be correlated with the
L768: response on the PASE questionnaires.
L769: 18
L770: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L771: Height, weight, seated and standing blood pressure, resting heart rate will be measured at screening
L772: session. Participants will undergo a clinical assessment with baseline to confirm current health status
L773: and eligibility for study using the PARQ+.
L774: The participant will be asked to bring an informant with them to complete the CDR. If the participant
L775: does not have an informant to attend the visit with them, the informant portion of the assessment can be
L776: completed with the informant by phone the day of the visit. This will be arranged and completed by a
L777: trained Research Assistant or Coordinator.
L778: 7.2 BASELINE EVALUATION (VISIT 2)
L779: Participants will undergo rigorous baseline evaluations, which will take place up to four weeks after the
L780: screening session. Baseline assessments will include neuroimaging, neuropsychological, and gait
L781: measures. These will be repeated at the 6-month and 12-month follow-up.
L782: 1. Log return of the Physical Activity Device
L783: 2. Blood Draw
L784: 3. Clinical Medical Questionnaire
L785: 4. Quality of Life Questionnaire (SF-36)
L786: 5. ADAS-Cog Plus Tests (ADAS-Cog 13 Tests, plus the Digit Span Forward and Backward WAIS
L787: III, TMT A and B, Digit Symbol Test, Boston Naming Test)
L788: 6. D-KEFS Colour Word Interference Test
L789: 7. Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)
L790: 8. Six Minute Walk Test (6MWT)
L791: 9. Dual task control assessment while seated (mental task)
L792: 10. Gait assessment using Gait Mat and Accelerometers (when available)
L793: 11. Schedule Neuroimaging
L794: 7.2.1 TREATMENT ALLOCATION
L795: **Randomization** Once informed consent has been obtained and the baseline assessment has been
L796: completed, participants will be allocated to each of the 5 arms. The randomization sequence of the
L797: participants will be generated in each site with a minimum of 4 participants recruited using a central,
L798: web-based randomization service. The Research Pharmacy will assign the ‘vitamin D/placebo kits’ in
L799: compliance with randomization lists generated by a computer program and allocation to groups will be
L800: done with sequentially numbered opaque sealed envelopes. A block randomization by 5 will be applied
L801: to ensure an appropriate balance of the characteristics of participants between each arm. Permuted
L802: 19
L803: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L804: blocks of varying size will be employed to ensure balance over time. After baseline assessment, research
L805: personnel not involved in measurement or intervention will access the web-based randomization service
L806: to determine the group allocation.
L807: 7.2.2 BLINDING
L808: In order to minimize a source of bias, this trial is planned to be a double-blinded study. Research
L809: personnel performing the outcome assessments will be blinded to group allocation. Participants will be
L810: blinded of intervention.
