L854: 7.6 STUDY PROCEDURE DESCRIPTIONS
L855: **7.6.1 Clinical-Medical Questionnaires**
L856: The presence of chronic diseases, comorbidities, chronic medications, history of previous falls will be
L857: recorded. Whenever possible, a family member will be also interviewed in order to confirm the medical
L858: history and functional status. Finally, fear of falling will be assessed using the Falls Efficacy Scale and
L859: the fear of falling scale.
L860: **7.6.2 Functionality score**
L861: Self-reported information will be collected at baseline, six and twelve month’s assessment on activities
L862: of daily living (ADLs) than are related to cognitive and physical capabilities. Specifically, the
L863: Alzheimer's Disease Cooperative Study Activities of Daily Living (ADCS-ADL) Inventory and IADL
L864: and Lawton Brody Instrumental Activities Of Daily Living (IADL) Scale will be used.
L865: 21
L866: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L867: **7.6.3 Blood Biomarkers**
L868: The following biomarkers will be drawn at the Baseline Visit,Visit 3 and Visit 4:
L869:   - Vitamin D levels, Calcium, PTH, and lipid level will be measured by conventional methods.
L870:   - Serum High Sensitive CRP, IL-6, BDNF, and VEGF receptor 1, IGF-1 will be determined by
L871: standardized ELISA methods
L872: Blood samples for this study (completed at baseline and 6-month visit) will be done by a certified study
L873: Research Coordinator or Research Assistant. Participants will be asked to fast and will be provided with
L874: a small breakfast after the draw is complete. Samples will be collected, processed and stored securely at
L875: the respective site in a -80C freezer used for research purposes. Samples will be batch shipped securely
L876: to a centralized laboratory for analysis. Sample tubes will be labeled appropriately by the Study
L877: Coordinator with only the participant’s unique study identifier and time point of collection.
L878: **7.6.4 Imaging**
L879: The imaging protocol will follow the _Canadian Dementia Imaging Protocol_ developed for CCNA by
L880: Dr. Simon Duchesne, Laval University and colleagues, the latter having already been used by members
L881: of the _Consortium pour l’identification précoce de la maladie d’Alzheimer- Québec (CIMA-Q)_ Pre-post
L882: regional patterns of brain plasticity will be assessed using structural (high-resolution 3D T1-weighted
L883: images = 7 min) in order to get voxel-based volumetric and cortical thickness measures, as well as
L884: diffusion tensor imaging (DTI, 30 directions, with AP/PA correction scan = 7 min) to calculate
L885: fractional anisotropy and diffusivity in white matter tracts as well as derive tract-based statistics.
L886: Cerebrovascular integrity and pathology will be assessed using the following contrasts: PD/T2 = 5 min;
L887: FLAIR = 7 min, and T2* = 5min). Functional magnetic resonance imaging at rest (rsfMRI plus field
L888: map = 12 min) will also be acquired to measure change in data-driven functional networks. Metabolite
L889: levels will be measured from the right motor cortex, cingulate cortex, and hippocampus, using single
L890: voxel spectroscopy. The total time for the scan session including scanning and patient setup is normally
L891: less than 1.5 hours.
L892: 7.6.5 COGNITIVE OUTCOMES
L893: **Alzheimer Disease Assessment Scale- Plus (** ADAS-Cog Plus **)**
L894: Global cognition will be assessed using the cognitive section of the Alzheimer Disease Assessment
L895: Scale Plus (ADAS-Cog Plus)[25]. The scale consists of 10 brief cognitive tests assessing memory,
L896: language, executive function and praxis. Scores range from 0 to 90, with higher scores indicating higher
L897: severity of cognitive impairment. The ADAS-Cog has been a significant outcome measure in numerous
L898: 22
L899: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L900: trials with MCI and AD. The ADAS-Cog Plus has marked advantages as an outcome measure in MCI
L901: populations since incorporates items concerning executive function and functionality. Reliability and
L902: validity has been confirmed. Since several items are harmonized with the classical ADAS-Cog, it will
L903: also provide comparability with previous or existing trials assessing intervention in MCI. The ADAS
L904: Cog Plus includes additional testing in executive function and caregiver reported functionality.
L905: Executive function of the ADAS-Cog plus is composed by the following tests: Trail-Making Test
L906: (TMT) A & B, the WAIS-R Digit Symbol Substitution Test (DSST), Digit Span Test, and Category
L907: Fluency.
L908: In brief, The TMT (A & B) assesses attention, speed, and mental flexibility. For this test, participants
L909: connect numbers (TMT-A) and numbers and letters (TMT-B) in order. Time to complete is the outcome
L910: of interest for this measure. Trails A is truncated at 3 min and Trails B at 5 min. The DSST measures
L911: speed and information processing. Participants are instructed to refer to a number-symbol key at the top
L912: of the page and to write the appropriate symbols under the corresponding numbers as quickly as
L913: possible. For the Digit Span Test, an auditory attention task, participants are asked to recall a series of
L914: numbers forward and backward. For Category Fluency, a measure of speed and flexibility of verbal
L915: thought, participants are asked to name as many items as possible in a specified category (vegetables);
L916: unique responses during the first minute were counted. Functionality will be assessed by the following
L917: five functionality questions : 1) “Ability of writing checks, paying bills, balancing checkbook;” 2)
L918: “Assembling tax records, business affairs, or papers”; 3) “Playing a game of skill, working on a hobby;”
L919: 4) “Keeping track of current events;” and 5) “Remembering appointments, family occasions, holidays,
L920: medications.”
L921: **Trail Making Test (TMT) A & B (s)**
L922: The Trail Making Test (TMT) measures set shifting abilities. Both parts of the test consist of 25 circles
L923: distributed across a sheet of paper. For the first portion of the test (TMT A), the participant is required
L924: to draw lines to connect numbers in ascending order as quickly as possible. This part assesses visual
L925: perception and psychomotor rapidity. TMT B assesses mental shifting and the participant’s attention
L926: ability, as they are required to do the same task as for TMT A, but alternating between numbers and
L927: letters. The participant is asked to perform the task as quickly as possible without lifting his/her pen
L928: from the paper. If the experimenter sees a mistake, she/he informs the participant. Results for both TMT
L929: A and B are reported as the number of seconds required to complete each task; therefore, higher scores
L930: reveal greater impairment. Trail A ≤ 40 seconds and Trail B ≤ 90 seconds are considered normal average
L931: values for these tests for older adults in the range of 60 to 75 years old. To index set shifting, the
L932: 23
L933: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L934: difference between part B and part A completion times, smaller difference scores indicate better set
L935: shifting. [46]
L936: **Digit Span Test WAIS-III (Forwards & Backwards)**
L937: These tests measure short term and working memory. For the Digit Forward, participants are instructed
L938: to listen to each series of numbers in completion, followed by repeating the numbers in the same order.
L939: The final score is the sum of points from each correct trial. The maximum score is 16. For the Digit
L940: Backward, participants are instructed to listen to each series of numbers in completion, followed by
L941: repeating the numbers in the backward order. The final score is the sum of the points from each correct
L942: trial/series, with a maximum score of 14. The difference between the Digit Span Forward and Backward
L943: test scores is used as an index of the central executive component of working memory. Smaller
L944: difference scores indicate better working memory.[47,48]
L945: **Boston Naming Test**
L946: This test measures confrontational word retrieval skills. This version is composed by thirty pictures that
L947: are shown by the experimenter one by one. Participants are asked to name verbally each of the 15 nouns
L948: depicted in the black and white drawings. Following an error in naming, a semantic and/or phonemic
L949: cue is provided verbally to participants wherein the participants are given up to 20 seconds to respond
L950: following each cue. This task will be digitally recorded and stored. If the participant is not able to
L951: retrieve the name even after the semantic cue the experimenter gives a phonemic cue by speaking the
L952: part in bold (e.g. **sc** roll; Experimenter says: “sc”).
L953: **Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7 (GAD-7)**
L954: The Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7 is a self-rated measure of anxiety symptoms and its score is
L955: calculated by rating 0, 1, 2, and 3 to the response categories of “not at all”, “several days”, “more than
L956: half the days” and “nearly every day”, respectively, and adding together the scores for the seven
L957: questions. A score of 10 or higher has a sensitivity of 89% and a specificity of 82% for generalized
L958: anxiety disorder; a score of 5 is considered the cut-off for “mild” anxiety and 15 for “severe” anxiety. [45 ]
L959: **Geriatric Depression Scale (GDS-30)**
L960: The Geriatric Depression Scale was designed to discriminate the pattern of depressive symptoms from
L961: the general characteristics of the elderly population. It is a brief 30-item questionnaire in which
L962: participants are asked to respond by answering yes or no in reference to how they felt over the past
L963: week. The GDS was found to have a 92% sensitivity and a 89% specificity when evaluated against
L964: diagnostic criteria. Cut-off: normal-0-9; mild depressives-10-19; severe depressives-20-30.[49]
L965: **Mini Mental State Examination (MMSE)**
L966: 24
L967: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L968: A 30 point questionnaire used to assess mental status. The MMSE tests 5 areas of cognitive function:
L969: orientation, registration, attention and calculation, and recall and language.
L970: **Montreal Cognitive Assessment Test (MoCA)**
L971: The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) was designed as a rapid screening instrument for mild
L972: cognitive dysfunction. It assesses different cognitive domains: attention and concentration, executive
L973: functions, memory, language, visuoconstructional skills, conceptual thinking, calculations, and
L974: orientation. Time to administer the MoCA is approximately 10 minutes. The total possible score is 30
L975: points; a score of 26 or above is considered normal. [41]
L976: 7.6.6 MOBILITY OUTCOMES
L977: **Gait velocity and variability (single and dual-tasking** )
L978: Gait velocity will be assessed as the time taken to walk the middle 6 meters of 8 meters using the
L979: Portable Walkway System (ProtoKinetic® and/or GAITRite® Systems, Inc.) These devices automate
L980: the acquisition, analysis, and reporting of the objective parameters of gait as the subject walks down the
L981: walkway with embedded sensors. As a result, these gait mats allow detecting subtle changes in gait,
L982: including gait variability since it has been proven to have a fine discrimination when compared with
L983: other quantitative methods.[50] The gait mat is located in a well-lit room with start and end points
L984: marked on the floor one meter from either end of the mat to avoid recording acceleration and
L985: deceleration phases.
L986: _Instrumentation_ _**:**_
L987: Gait performance will be assessed using electronic walkway systems (GAITRite® or PkMas®), which
L988: automatically determines spatiotemporal gait parameters from imbedded sensors activated by foot
L989: pressure. A computer processes the footsteps, providing data for both spatial and temporal parameters.
L990: In addition, among sites without electronic walkway systems, quantitative gait parameters will be
L991: assessed using wearable inertial sensors (Gulf Coast Inc.; Shimmer Inc.) worn bilaterally on the ankles
L992: and at the hip. Acceleration data will be used to calculate foot-contact and foot-off times determine step
L993: and stride timing data. Overall gait velocity is determined by timing the standard walk distance.
L994: For walking conditions all individuals will perform walks along a 6 meter path while wearing hip and
L995: ankle worn accelerometers. Individuals will perform three main tasks: 1) preferred walking speed, 2)
L996: dual task walking and 3) fast walking. In the case that a participant uses an assistive aid (cane, walker),
L997: the tester will determine the participant’s capacity to walk safely without the use of the aid. With the
L998: 25
L999: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L1000: willingness of the participant, they will then perform one test walk without the use of the aid. If the
L1001: participant is able to safely walk without the use of the assistive aid, all subsequent testing will be
L1002: performed without its use. In all walks, participants will start 1 meter before the beginning of the 6 meter
L1003: platform and continue to travel 1 meter past the end of the platform. This procedure is in place to ensure
L1004: steady-state walking and to minimize any effects of acceleration and de-acceleration during the course
L1005: of the walk.
L1006: The first walking block will measure self-selected or preferred walking. Participants will be instructed to
L1007: “walk at a comfortable and secure pace”. For preferred walking speed, a total of three walks will be
L1008: performed. Participants with slow walking speed, less than 0.6m/s, or participants with lower limb
L1009: disability, will be allowed to complete one walk if they are not able to perform the three trials. We
L1010: require a minimum of approximately 20-steps to allow measurement of step-to-step variability. For
L1011: example, an individual who walks at 1.5 m/s would usually only have 4-5 steps per walk. Therefore, we
L1012: would need at least 3 repetitions. For those walking less than 1.0 m/s, two trials is commonly necessary.
L1013: For those walking less than 0.5 m/s, a single walk is usually sufficient.
L1014: The second walking block will be dual-task walking and includes three separate walks. Before
L1015: performing the walking task, participants will enumerate out loud the verbal outputs will be performed
L1016: from a seated position prior to any walks and recorded. The seated assessments will be timed at 10
L1017: seconds for dual-tasks and will be performed in the beginning of all cognitive assessments to prevent
L1018: practice effects in dual-task gait performance (see order of assessments for visit 2, 3 and 4). If during the
L1019: assessment time restriction appears or the assessor can see the tasks are difficult for the individual, the
L1020: minimum gait assessment required will be usual gait, naming animals and Serial 7s. The first dual-task
L1021: administered will have the participant walk on the platform while simultaneously counting backward by
L1022: serial 1’s from 100 (100-99-98…) out loud. The second dual-task will have the participant repeat the
L1023: walk but this time, while naming animals (fluency test). In the third dual-task, the participant will be
L1024: asked to walk the platform while subtracting 7’s from 100 (100-93-86…) out loud. During this trial,
L1025: participants are encouraged to keep walking even if they cannot do the subtractions. These dual-task
L1026: conditions selected are based on previous research which demonstrated that counting backwards requires
L1027: both working memory and attention[51]. Participants will be instructed to pay attention to both gait and
L1028: the cognitive task; if a participant stops either task during the trial, they will be prompted to continue.
L1029: Not instructing to prioritize gait over cognitive task or vice-versa allows both gait and cognitive task to
L1030: vary. This has previously been shown to provide a better representation of what happens naturally
L1031: [52,53]. The evaluator will record any counting errors during walking.
L1032: 26
L1033: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L1034: The third and last walking block will include fast walking and the participants will be instructed to walk
L1035: as fast and safe as they can without running.
L1036: **Six-minute Walking Test (6MWT)**
L1037: The 6MWT is used to quantitatively measure participants’ exercise capacity by measuring the maximum
L1038: distance that a person can walk in 6 minutes. The test is a modification of the 12-min walk/run test
L1039: originally developed by Cooper [54] and is commonly used as an assessment of exercise capacity. The
L1040: 6MWT is useful for predicting the maximal oxygen uptake related to cardiorespiratory fitness and is
L1041: easily administered in clinical settings [55]. The 6MWT is believed to be a useful instrument because of
L1042: its ease of administration and similarity to normal daily activities. It is a submaximal test of aerobic
L1043: capacity which has been successfully used in MCI populations [56].
L1044: The 6MWT will be assessed by licensed and trained physical therapists. The participants are instructed
L1045: to walk from one end of a 10-meter course to the other and back again as many times as possible in 6
L1046: min, while under the supervision of a physical therapist. After each minute, participants were informed
L1047: of the time elapsed and were given standardized encouragement. The distance (meters) walked in 6 min
L1048: was recorded [55].
L1049: **Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB)**
L1050: It is a widely used instrument for quantifying lower extremity function and mobility in older adults. It is
L1051: a group of measures that combines the results of the gait speed, chair stand and balance tests [57]. It
L1052: has been used as a predictive tool for possible disability and can aid in the monitoring of function in
L1053: older people. The scores range from 0 (worst performance) to 12 (best performance). The SPPB has
L1054: been shown to have predictive validity showing a gradient of risk for mortality, nursing home
L1055: admission, and disability. [58]
L1056: 7.6.7 FUNCTIONAL OUTCOMES AND COMBINED SCORE OF COGNITION AND
L1057: FUNCTIONALITY (POOLED INDEX)
L1058: Functional Outcomes: Activities of daily living and everyday function will be evaluated using the use
L1059: the ADCS which is a 23-item informant-rated questionnaire that measures, in a range of 0 to 78, an
L1060: individual’s performance of activities of daily living.
L1061: Pooled Index: A pool index including measures of cognition, mobility and functionality will be used as a
L1062: secondary outcome. The cognitive decline seen with aging, due to neurodegenerative or vascular
L1063: mechanisms, affects not only cognition but also mood, behavior and the ability to perform activities of
L1064: 27
L1065: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L1066: daily living. Therefore, treatments are considered successful if they slow down the progression of
L1067: cognitive decline and maintain functionality and independency. Studies conducted in psychiatric patients
L1068: and therapies for rheumatoid arthritis have demonstrated the applicability of a pooled index (PI) in
L1069: measuring overall function and how changes in PI scores can be analyzed to quantify improvement or
L1070: worsening conditions over time[60-62]. To apply this approach in our cohort, we constructed the
L1071: **Cognition and Functionality Pooled Index** with six continuous variables sensitive to cognition
L1072: function in older adults: Trail- Making Test, parts A and B (executive function), Rey Auditory Verbal
L1073: Learning Test (RAVLT) (semantic memory, verbal learning), Geriatric Depression Scale [GDS] (mood),
L1074: Alzheimer’s disease Activities of Daily Living-International Scale [ADL-IS] (functionality), and dual
L1075: task gait cost. The efficiency of a Pool Index is greatest when the combined variables are poorly
L1076: correlated (r<0.4) which reduces variance of PI. The suitability of our 6 source variables was confirmed
L1077: by calculating the strength of pairwise correlations using Pearson Correlation Coefficient. The pooled
L1078: index is created by converting all individual scores from the six tests to standardized _z_ scores by
L1079: subtracting the baseline group mean and dividing by the baseline group SD, and then averaging the
L1080: standardized _z_ scores. For tests with more than 1 component, the component scores were first averaged
L1081: to create a single _z_ score for each test (e.g., _z_ scores for Trails A and B were averaged to create a single
L1082: Trails _z_ score) so that all tests were weighted equally, and then the mean _z_ scores for the 6 tests was
L1083: calculated.
L1084: 7.6.8 QUALITY OF LIFE OUTCOMES
L1085: Health related quality of life will be evaluated using the SF 36 that provides weightings for quality
L1086: adjusted life year (QALYs). The SF 36 captures health states based on the following domains: 1)
L1087: mobility; 2) self-care; 3) usual activities; 4) pain and 5) anxiety or depression. We will calculate scores
L1088: using the established methodology to determine if there is a statistically significant difference in the
L1089: incremental cost per QALY change across the 5 arms.
L1090: 7.6.9 HEALTH RESOURCE UTILIZATION
L1091: At Visits 2 and 3, we will ask participants to report any visit to: 1) health care professionals; 2)
L1092: admissions or visits to hospital; and 3) laboratory work using the health resource utilization
L1093: questionnaire. This questionnaire has been previously described and supported in previous studies
L1094: [Neumann PJ, Goldie SJ, Weinstein MC: Preference-based measures in economic evaluation in health
L1095: care. Annual review of public health. 2000, 21: 587-611. 10.1146/annurev.publhealth.21.1.587.]. We
L1096: will estimate total health care related costs over the 12 months from a Canadian health care system
L1097: 28
L1098: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L1099: perspective. Participants will be instructed to specify total health care expenditure and report the reason
L1100: for each item. Additionally, participants will be instructed to report health care expenditure due to any
L1101: adverse events associated with our study. On a per participant basis, costs will be assigned to health
L1102: care resource utilization using a fully allocated hospital cost model (for in-patient costs) and provincial
L1103: guides to medical fees (for outpatient costs). Case analyses will consider the costs of all health care
L1104: resource use and our sensitivity analyses will include only intervention related health care resource costs
L1105: and a complete case analysis.
L1106: **8. ANALYSIS**
L1107: **Analysis, planned analysis, frequency of analysis, and correction for multiple testing**
L1108: Analysis will be conducted as intention to treat and as per-protocol analysis. For the primary endpoint
L1109: (ADAS-Cog plus), the change from baseline to six months and 12 months will be assessed using a
L1110: repeated measures analysis of covariance (ANCOVA) model incorporating the baseline values as
L1111: covariates. Observing a statistically significant difference in the primary outcome will be considered
L1112: preliminary evidence of efficacy. Additionally, reduction of prevalence rate of abnormal ADAS-Cog
L1113: plus score (dichotomous variable) will be asses as a primary outcome.
L1114: We will also report variances, co-variances, and effect sizes, as well as sampling feasibility (i.e., ease of
L1115: recruitment, recruitment rate, withdrawal rate). Descriptive statistics for demographic and baseline
L1116: characteristics will be provided with means and standard deviations, or medians and the interquartile
L1117: range where appropriate, for continuous characteristics and frequencies and percentages for categorical
L1118: variables.
L1119: If the within-group comparison is statistically significant, Tukey’s multiple comparisons will identify
L1120: significant pairwise differences. Similarly, for the secondary endpoints, six month changes in gait
L1121: performance and secondary cognitive outcomes of interest will be performed using one way analyses of
L1122: variance. For group comparisons that are statistically significant, pairwise comparisons will be made
L1123: using Tukey’s multiple comparisons tests. All statistical tests will be two-tailed, and a _p_ -value of less
L1124: than 0.05 will be considered to indicate statistical significance. All calculations will be made using SPSS
L1125: software package version 23.0 (SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL).
L1126: Secondary analyses will be also performed according to an intention-to-treat principle and per protocol
L1127: analysis. Frequency of the analyses: preliminary analysis will be performed at month 6 to ascertain
L1128: descriptive characteristics at baseline assessment. Final efficacy analysis will be performed at the end of
L1129: the trial since no safety issues are involved in this study. The statistical significance of the results will be
L1130: 29
L1131: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L1132: determined by Hochberg's variation of the Bonferroni procedure for multiple testing. [39] An economical
L1133: analysis will be also performed addressing health resources utilization.
L1134: **9. ADVERSE EVENT (AE) AND SERIOUS ADVERSE EVENT REPORTING (SAE)**
L1135: Adverse events will be recorded for subjects starting at the time of signing the Informed Consent and
L1136: their discontinuation within the study. All AEs will be recorded on an ongoing paper log, which will be
L1137: reviewed by each site leader and forwarded to the team leader at London site, who will determine both
L1138: the intensity of the event and the relationship of the event to study procedures. Based on the outcome it
L1139: will be up to the team leader to medically determine if it is justified for the participant to continue in the
L1140: study or terminate their participation.
L1141: SAEs will be recorded on a paper log, starting at the time of signing the Informed Consent and up to the
L1142: end of their participation within the study. SAEs which are classified as unanticipated will be reported
L1143: to local REBs. An SAE is defined as any incident that is unexpected, and related or possibly related to
L1144: participation in the research study. SAEs will be reviewed and monitored by each site team leader until
L1145: resolved.
L1146: 9.1 CONCOMITANT MEDICATIONS
L1147: Antacids (Magnesium-containing): Hypermagnesemia may develop when these agents are used
L1148: concurrently with Vitamin D, particularly in patients with chronic renal failure.
L1149: Anticonvulsants (Phenytoin, Phenobarbital): Decreased Vitamin D effects may occur when certain
L1150: anticonvulsants are administered, as they may induce hepatic microsomial enzymes and accelerate the
L1151: conversion of Vitamin D to inactive metabolites.
L1152: Cholestyramine, Colestipol, Mineral Oil: Intestinal absorption of Vitamin D may be impaired. Patients
L1153: on cholestyramine or colestipol should be advised to allow as much time as possible between the
L1154: ingestion of these drugs and Vitamin D.
L1155: Digoxin: Vitamin D should be used with caution in patients on digoxin as hypercalcemia (which may
L1156: result with Vitamin D use) may precipitate cardiac arrhythmias.
L1157: Thiazide diuretics: There is an increased risk of hypercalcaemia if vitamin D is coadministered with
L1158: thiazide diuretics and calcium. Plasma-calcium concentrations should be monitored in patients receiving
L1159: the drugs concurrently (Source: Martindale).
L1160: Different Vitamin D analogues should be administered concurrently.
L1161: 30
L1162: CCNA MEC Team Protocol Version: 15 February 2019
L1163: 9.2 RESCUE MEDICATION AND RISK MANAGEMENT
L1164: Higher doses of Vitamin D may lead to hypervitaminosis D manifested by hypercalcemia and its
L1165: sequelae. Treatment of acute or chronic intoxication includes withdrawal of Vitamin D3 and any
L1166: calcium supplements, maintenance of a low calcium diet and if needed corticosteroids or calciuric
L1167: diuretics, such as furosemide and ethacrymic acid to decrease serum calcium concentrations. All
L1168: participants will be monitored by site physicians and trained staff should any adverse events arise
L1169: participants will have access to a phone number and e-mail address provided on the copy of their signed
L1170: consent form. They will be informed to use these contacts to inform the Principal Investigator about
L1171: concerns that may be associated with treatment received in each site.
L1172: 9.3 PREMATURE WITHDRAWAL/DISCONTINUATION CRITERIA
L1173: Participants may be withdrawn or be discontinued from the study for any of the following reasons:
L1174: 1. At the participant’s request (withdrawal of consent)
L1175: 2. At the discretion of the investigator, if deemed appropriate, for any reason
L1176: 3. If an intolerable or life threatening adverse event occurs
L1177: Or if any of the following develop:
L1178: 4. renal dysfunction
L1179: 5. dementia
L1180: 6. severe depression/anxiety/schizophrenia
L1181: 7. hypercalcaemia and/or hypermagnesemia
L1182: Participant data collected to the date of discontinuation or withdrawal will be included in the analysis.
L1183: **10. ETHICAL CONSIDERATIONS**
L1184: This study will be conducted in compliance with the outlined protocol, International Conference on
L1185: Harmonization Good Clinical Practice (ICH-GCP), and all applicable regulatory requirements.
L1186: All relevant documents for this study will be submitted to the appropriate Research Ethics Board (REB)
L1187: for review. Written approval from the REB must be obtained prior to entering and testing participants
L1188: within this study. The REB will be notified of any protocol amendments regarding new study
L1189: procedures which may occur during the course of the study. Written approval from the REB on these
L1190: amendments must be received prior to implementation of any new procedures.
