L1597: ## 97 7. Determination of Sample Size
L1598: 98 Sample size calculation is based on changes in our primary outcome: global cognitive
L1599: 99 function measured using the Alzheimer Disease Assessment Scale Cognitive 13 and
L1600: 4
L1601: SYNERGIC Trial                                                    Statistical Analyses Plan
L1602: 100 the plus variant (ADAS-Cog 13 and plus). A target sample of 200 participants (40 per
L1603: 101 arm) was determined based on 80% power to detect either main effects or an
L1604: 102 interaction of at least a moderate (Cohen’s d ~0.5) [1] effect size of changes in the ADAS103 Cog-13. [2] The final sample size accounted for a 15% attrition.
L1605: 104
L1606: ## 105 8. Primary Outcomes
L1607: 106 Primary outcome will be changes in cognitive function measured with the ADAS-Cog 13
L1608: 107 items (ADAS-Cog-13) and the plus variant (ADAS-Cog-plus) at endpoint, 6 Month.
L1609: 108 Improvement at endpoint in either variant is considered evidence of efficacy.
L1610: ### 109 8.1 Change in cognitive function on ADAS-Cog-13 at post-intervention (Month 6)
L1611: 110 The ADAS-Cog 13 consists of 13 brief cognitive tests assessing memory, language,
L1612: 111 attention, concentration and praxis. The individual tests are listed in Table 2. Scores
L1613: 112 range from 0 to 84, with higher scores indicating severe cognitive impairment.
L1614: ### 113 8.2 Change in cognitive function on ADAS-Cog-Plus at post-intervention (Month 6)
L1615: 114 Four additional tests focusing on executive function will be added to the ADAS-Cog 13
L1616: 115 to comprise the plus variant [3] (Table 2). Scores will be transformed into an algorithm
L1617: 116 validated by Crane et al. [4] The higher scores will indicate severe of cognitive impairment.
L1618: 117
L1619: 118 Table 2. Individual tests included in the ADAS-Cog
L1620: 5
L1621: |ADAS-Cog-13|ADAS-Cog-Plus|
L1622: |---|---|
L1623: |Word recall|Word recall|
L1624: |Delayed word recall|Delayed word recall|
L1625: |Following commands|Following commands|
L1626: |Constructional praxis|Constructional praxis|
L1627: |Ideational praxis|Ideational praxis|
L1628: |Naming objects|Naming objects|
L1629: |Orientations|Orientations|
L1630: |Word recognition|Word recognition|
L1631: |Remembering instructions|Remembering instructions|
L1632: |Comprehension|Comprehension|
L1633: |Word finding|Word finding|
L1634: |Spoken language|Spoken language|
L1635: |Number cancellation|Number cancellation|
L1636: ||Trial Making A and B|
L1637: ||Digit symbol substitution|
L1638: ||WAIS III digit forward and backward|
L1639: ||Category fluency – Animals and Vegetable|
L1640: SYNERGIC Trial                                                    Statistical Analyses Plan
L1641: ## 119 9. Secondary Outcomes [*]
L1642: 120 Secondary outcomes include neuropsychological assessments, gait and mobility
L1643: 121 outcomes (including incidence of fall), neuroimaging, and blood biomarkers. *Note that
L1644: 122 secondary outcomes will be not reported in the primary manuscript.
L1645: ### 123 9.1 Change in cognitive function on neuropsychological tests
L1646: 124 The secondary cognitive outcomes will include:
L1647: 125 a) Montreal Cognitive Assessment test for global cognition
L1648: 126 b) The ADAS-Cog Recall word list for verbal semantic memory
L1649: 127 c) Trail Making Test A and B
L1650: 128 d) Digit Symbol Substitution Test
L1651: 129 e) Digit Span Test Forward and Backwards
L1652: 130 f) Boston Naming Test
L1653: 131 g) Verbal fluency Animals and Vegetables
L1654: ### 132 9.2 Change in gait performance
L1655: 133 Gait performance will be evaluated using gait speed and gait variability under single and
L1656: 134 dual-tasking. Gait speed will be measured as the time taken to walk 6 m using an
L1657: 135 electronic walkway system (ProtoKinetic® and/or GAITRite® Systems, Inc.). Gait
L1658: 136 variability of spatial and temporal gait variables (stride time, stride length, double
L1659: 137 support time and step width) will be calculated using the coefficient of variation (CV =
L1660: 138 (standard deviation / mean) ×100).
L1661: ### 139 9.3 Change in mobility and falls
L1662: 140 Mobility will be measured with the Short Physical Performance Battery (SPPB), and the
L1663: 141 6 min walk test (6MWT).
L1664: 142 A fall will be defined as ‘unintentionally coming to rest on the ground, floor, or other
L1665: 143 lower level and not due to a seizure, syncope, or an acute stroke’. Recurrent falls are
L1666: 144 defined as ‘two or more events in a 12-month period’. Falls will be recorded throughout
L1667: 145 the 12-month trial, participants will be provided with a falls calendars, on which they will
L1668: 146 record any falls that have occurred, and they will be asked to bring them monthly to the
L1669: 147 training sessions to review with a research staff member. After the completion of the
L1670: 148 intervention, participants will be contacted at month 9 and 12 to report incidental falls.
L1671: ### 149 9.4 Change in Neuroimaging
L1672: 150 Pre-post regional patterns of brain plasticity will be assessed using structural (high151 resolution 3D T1-weighted images = 7 min) in order to get voxel-based volumetric and
L1673: 152 cortical thickness measures, as well as diffusion tensor imaging (DTI, 30 directions, with
L1674: 6
L1675: SYNERGIC Trial                                                    Statistical Analyses Plan
L1676: 153 AP/PA correction scan = 7 min) to calculate fractional anisotropy and diffusivity in white
L1677: 154 matter tracts as well as derive tract-based statistics. Cerebrovascular integrity and
L1678: 155 pathology will be assessed using the following contrasts: PD/T2 = 5 min; FLAIR = 7 min,
L1679: 156 and T2* = 5 min). Functional magnetic resonance imaging at rest (rsfMRI plus field map
L1680: 157 = 12 min) will also be acquired to measure change in data-driven functional networks.
L1681: ### 158 9.5 Change in Blood biomarkers measures
L1682: 159 Serum biomarkers of inflammation (C reactive protein, and interleukin 6), neuroplasticity
L1683: 160 (brain-derived neurotropic factor), endothelial health (vascular endothelial growth factor
L1684: 161 1), and vitamin D serum levels will be measured before and after intervention.
L1685: ### 162 9.6 Covariates
L1686: 163 Pre-specified covariates will be age, sex, years of education and number of
L1687: 164 comorbidities.
L1688: 165
L1689: ## 166 10. Statistical Analysis
L1690: ### 167 10.1 Descriptive Analysis
L1691: 168 The baseline characteristics will be summarized using means and standard deviation, or
L1692: 169 medians and interquartile range where appropriate, for continuous characteristics, and
L1693: 170 frequencies and percentages for categorical variable.
L1694: ### 171 10.2 Primary Efficacy Analysis
L1695: 172 The primary analysis will be based on the intention-to-treat principle, including everyone
L1696: 173 who were randomized. Comparisons between arms will be conducted according to pre174 specifications in the protocol. These comparisons align well with the recommended
L1697: 175 analysis and reporting of factorial trials [5], which used “inside the table” and “at the
L1698: 176 margin” analyses for different comparisons. The “inside the table” analysis will be used
L1699: 177 to compare each intervention effect to control, while “at the margins” analysis will be
L1700: 178 used to assess the effect of adding intervention.
L1701: 179 The major interest would be the effect of the intervention on change in primary
L1702: 180 outcomes at 6 Month compared to the control arm (arm 5). The primary outcome
L1703: 181 measure of mean change in ADAS-Cog-13 and ADAS-Cog-Plus scores will be analyzed
L1704: 182 using a linear mixed model with restricted likelihood estimation. The model will be fitted
L1705: 183 with participant-specific random intercept and fixed effects of time, intervention arm, and
L1706: 184 time x intervention arm interaction. The time variable will be used as a categorical
L1707: 185 variable (0 = baseline; 1 = Month 6.) The intervention arm variable will be dummy
L1708: 186 coded, using the control group as a reference category. The interaction term will be
L1709: 7
L1710: SYNERGIC Trial                                                    Statistical Analyses Plan
L1711: 187 used to assess between-group differences (representing a mean difference in change in
L1712: 188 cognitive scores over time between two groups). With dummy coding of the intervention
L1713: 189 arm, the interaction term will allow to compare the mean change in each intervention
L1714: 190 arm to the arm 5 (control). The within-group difference (representing a mean change in
L1715: 191 cognitive scores over time within the group) will be measured with marginalized mean
L1716: 192 from the linear mixed regression model. The model will be further adjusted for
L1717: 193 participant characteristics including age, sex, education, and comorbidities.
L1718: 194 An unstructured covariance matrix will be used for all models. The model assumption
L1719: 195 will be assessed using the normal quantile plots of residuals for normality and the
L1720: 196 residuals vs. fitted plot for variance homogeneity. If the assumptions are not fully met,
L1721: 197 the data will be transformed (i.e., log). Missing data will not be imputed and will be
L1722: 198 assumed to be missing at random, as the mixed model approach is well-known to
L1723: 199 handle such data effectively. [6] The baseline data from individuals who did not complete
L1724: 200 Month 6 assessment will be included in the linear mixed model.
L1725: 201 The mean difference between groups will be presented with their 95% CI and p-value,
L1726: 202 while the mean change within group will be presented with their standard error. The
L1727: 203 obtained marginalized mean and standard deviation will be used to calculate the effect
L1728: ���� ������ �� ��������� � � ���� ������ �� ��������� �
L1729: 204 size ( ). As outlined in our published
L1730: ������ �������� ��������� �� ������
L1731: 205 protocol, we will calculate the proportion of participants with improvement in primary
L1732: 206 outcomes.
L1733: 207 All tests will be two-sided, and p-value of less than 0.05 will be considered as
L1734: 208 statistically significant. Adjustments for multiple comparisons will not be made as the
L1735: 209 trial is intended to compare the intervention effect to the control rather than pairwise. [7]
L1736: 210 Per-protocol analysis will be performed as well.
L1737: 211 As per recommended analyses of fractional factorial design [8], “at the margin” analyses
L1738: 212 will be performed by collapsing arms according to the pre-specified comparison.
L1739: 213 Collapsing intervention arms allow to increase the study power to detect the difference
L1740: 214 in effect. More details are provided below for each pre-specified comparison.
L1741: 215 10.2.1 Effect of aerobic-resistance exercise
L1742: 216 To address objective 1 (the effect of supervised aerobic-resistance exercise on
L1743: 217 cognitive function), the intervention arms providing exercise intervention (arm 1 to 4) will
L1744: 218 be collapsed as one category and compared to the control (arm 5). The new
L1745: 219 independent variable will be created to represent exercise intervention: aerobic220 resistance exercise intervention (collapsed arm 1 to 4), and control exercise intervention
L1746: 221 - BAT – (arm 5). The new intervention variable will be fitted into the same linear mixed
L1747: 222 model described above. Using the interaction between exercise intervention x time, the
L1748: 8
L1749: SYNERGIC Trial                                                    Statistical Analyses Plan
L1750: 223 change in primary outcome will be contrasted between participants receiving the
L1751: 224 aerobic-resistance exercise and those not receiving the aerobic-resistance exercise.
L1752: 225 10.2.2 Effect of adding cognitive training
L1753: 226 The objective 2 (the effect of adding cognitive training to aerobic-resistance exercise on
L1754: 227 cognitive function) will be examined by pooling the intervention arms providing both
L1755: 228 exercise and cognitive intervention (arm 1 and 2) and pooling the intervention arms
L1756: 229 providing exercise intervention but not cognitive intervention (arm 3 and 4). A new
L1757: 230 independent variable will be created for addition of cognitive training: both exercise and
L1758: 231 cognitive intervention (pooled arm 1 and 2) and exercise but no cognitive intervention
L1759: 232 (pooled arm 3 and 4). Using the interaction term in the linear mixed model, the contrast
L1760: 233 will be made between the two collapsed arms to assess whether the effect of adding
L1761: 234 cognitive training to aerobic-resistance exercise differ from aerobic-resistance exercise.
L1762: 235 If there is a significant difference observed, the two collapsed arms will be compared to
L1763: 236 the control arm (arm 5). This marginal analysis will allow to evaluate the main effect of
L1764: 237 aerobic-resistance exercise alone (pooled arm 1 and 2) and aerobic-resistance exercise
L1765: 238 with cognitive training (pooled arm 3 and 4) compared to the control (arm 5) with higher
L1766: 239 power (as a result of increase in sample size). [5,8]
L1767: 240 10.2.3 Effect of adding vitamin D supplements
L1768: 241 The objective 2 (the effect of adding vitamin D to aerobic-resistance exercise on
L1769: 242 cognitive function) will be examined by pooling the intervention arms providing both
L1770: 243 exercise and vitamin D intervention (arm 1 and 3) and pooling the intervention arms
L1771: 244 providing exercise intervention but not vitamin D (arm 2 and 4). A new independent
L1772: 245 variable will be created to represent addition of vitamin D: both aerobic-resistance
L1773: 246 exercise and vitamin D intervention (pooled arm 1 and 3) and aerobic-resistance
L1774: 247 exercise but no vitamin D intervention (pooled arm 2 and 4). The linear mixed model will
L1775: 248 be repeated using this new independent variable and the two pooled arms will be
L1776: 249 compared. This will allow to explore whether adding vitamin D to aerobic-resistance
L1777: 250 exercise improve the outcome compared to the aerobic-resistance exercise. If a
L1778: 251 significant difference is observed, the two pooled arms will be compared to the control
L1779: 252 arm (arm 5) to assess the main effect.
L1780: 253 10.2.4 Effect of multidomain intervention
L1781: 254 The objective 4 (effect of multi-domain intervention (aerobic-resistance exercise +
L1782: 255 cognitive training + Vitamin D supplement) on cognitive function) will be assessed using
L1783: 256 the time x intervention arm interaction fitted in the linear mixed model in section 10.2.
L1784: 257 The interaction term between time x intervention arm 1 will represents the effect of
L1785: 258 multidomain intervention (arm 1) compared to the control (arm 5).
L1786: 9
L1787: SYNERGIC Trial                                                    Statistical Analyses Plan
L1788: 259 10.2.5 Long-term effect: From Baseline and Post-intervention to Follow up
L1789: 260 The long-term intervention effect from baseline and post-intervention to follow-up
L1790: 261 (Month 12) will be assessed using the linear mixed model. Time will be used as a
L1791: 262 categorical variable (Baseline, Month 6, and Month 12). Individuals with incomplete
L1792: 263 follow up assessments will still be included in the linear mixed model.
L1793: 264 10.2.6 Post-hoc analyses
L1794: 265 ADAS-Cog-Plus is a construction proposed originally by Skinner et al. [9], adding extra
L1795: 266 tests for executive function and verbal fluency to increase sensitivity to detect
L1796: 267 progression from MCI to dementia. Post hoc analyses will be performed to explore the
L1797: 268 effect of intervention on ADAS-Cog-Plus scores by adding individual Plus items, one by
L1798: 269 one, to ADAS-Cog-13.
L1799: ### 270 10.3 Secondary Outcomes Analyses
L1800: 271 The secondary analysis will be performed in an intention-to-treat population and a per272 protocol population. The details of the statistical analysis plan for secondary outcomes
L1801: 273 are not included in this analysis plan as the secondary outcome analyses will not be
L1802: 274 included in this primary manuscript and performed afterwards.
L1803: 275
L1804: ## 276 11. Data management and Quality Control
L1805: 277 All sites will use the same data collection forms to maintain consistency. Data storage
L1806: 278 and monitoring procedures will be performed using the LORIS (Longitudinal Online
L1807: 279 Research and Imaging System) system platform for uploading data specially developed
L1808: 280 for the SYNERGIC study by CCNA informatics team platform. Data quality will be
L1809: 281 assessed either using Excel and the online CCNA-LORIS platform database. All data
L1810: 282 will be entered and managed in LORIS database, and a double data entry system will
L1811: 283 be used to assure accuracy of entry. Outliers and missing data will be managed using
L1812: 284 statistical software adopted for the final analysis. Original completed CRFs will be
L1813: 285 uploaded to the media module, initial and double data entries will be performed, and
L1814: 286 arising conflicts will be resolved in the conflict resolver module. Once the conflicts are
L1815: 287 resolved, the data will be validated by the clinical team and will be ready for release
L1816: 288 through the data query tool.
L1817: 289 See: https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fninf.2018.00085/full
L1818: 290
L1819: 291
