L2172: **eMethods.**
L2173: _**MCI Alberta criteria**_ _**[1]**_ _**and operationalization in the SYNERGIC trial**_
L2174: |Criterion|Operationalization|
L2175: |---|---|
L2176: |i) Subjective cognitive complains|Self-reported and/or by an informant|
L2177: |ii) Objective cognitive impairment in<br>**1** of the following 4 cognitive<br>domains: memory, executive<br>function, attention, and language|1 or more of the following:<br>- Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) scores<br>ranging from: 13-24/30<br>- Logical Memory below Alzheimer’s Disease<br>Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI) cut-offs: <9 for 16+<br>years of education; <5 for 8-15 years of education; <3<br>for 0-7 years of education.<br>- Consortium to Establish a Registry for Alzheimer’s<br>Disease (CERAD) word list recall < 6.|
L2178: |iii) Preserved activities of daily living|Score >14/23 on the Lawton-Brody Instrumental<br>Activities Of Daily Living (IADL)|
L2179: |iv) Absence of dementia|- Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental<br>Disorders (V Edition)<br>and/or<br>- Global Clinical Dementia Rating (CDR) ≤ 0.5|
L2180: _**Details of Cognitive training intervention**_
L2181: Cognitive Training (CT) intervention will involve tablet-based multimodal and multi-domain dual-task training
L2182: with memory load. The training uses the custom-written program, developed for neuro- rehabilitation and used
L2183: in previous research trials for cognitive [2-4] and mobility outcomes. [5] Training sessions will take place in groups of
L2184: up to eight participants before each of the fitness-training session for duration of 30 min max. Participants will
L2185: perform a concurrent visuo-motor task (dual-task combination) composed of different sets of visual stimuli that
L2186: have to be identified by tapping designated figures on an iPad (or an Android tablet). Participants will perform
L2187: discrimination tasks involving sets of items (e.g. letters, numbers, animals, vehicles, fruits, celestial bodies).
L2188: Each session will involve two sets of items, one for each task. The combination of sets of items will change
L2189: every four sessions.
L2190: Task instructions will always be administered by a trained Research Assistant. Within each training session and
L2191: for each task combination, participants will complete a first block of single-pure (SP) trials followed by mixedtrial blocks with single-task trials (SM) intermixed and dual-mixed trials (DM: 2 tasks at once). Each training
L2192: session will involve 80 SP, 128 SM and 192 DM trials (thus 40% of the DM blocks will be SM trials). Within
L2193: the DM trials, the first 30 training sessions will be performed following a fixed priority instruction (participants
L2194: will be asked to keep an equal priority on left and right hands.) The following 30 sessions will be performed
L2195: following a variable priority instruction (participants will be told to vary priority from one hand to another in
L2196: different blocks). Participants will complete a total of 60 sessions. Importantly, an adaptive continuous feedback
L2197: on performance was will be provided during the dual-mixed block of the training sessions. Feedback will take
L2198: the shape of a speedometer whose indicator moves and changes colour (green, yellow, orange or red) to inform
L2199: participants of their response speed. Two speedometers (one for each hand) will be displayed at the top of the
L2200: screen. Each speedometer is associated to one task. In equal priority blocks, participants will be asked to try to
L2201: maintain both speedometers at least in the yellow zone and prevent them from turning to red. In variable
L2202: priority blocks, participants will be asked to maintain the speedometer associated to the prioritized task in the
L2203: green zone, while the other speedometer can be at least in the orange zone. The colour of the speedometers will
L2204: be determined by the average reaction time (RT) on the last three trials’ RT for the DM block compared to the
L2205: median RT for the SM trials multiplied by a factor of 1.5 (for the first training session, as there are no previous
L2206: © 2023 Montero-Odasso M et al. _JAMA Network Open._
L2207: SM trials, the DM block will be compared to 1500ms). In addition, a feedback will be provided at the end of the
L2208: session, where participants will be informed of mean RT and accuracy achieved throughout sessions (presented
L2209: in a histogram without explicit values).
L2210: _**Details of Control Cognitive training**_
L2211: The active control cognitive training group will participate in skills training courses based on a published study
L2212: from our research group. [6] Each session will consist of Internet search for 3 hotels, 3 touristic places and 3
L2213: restaurants, of participant’s preference, in cities determined by the instructor at the beginning of each training
L2214: session (a list of cities is presented in the manual of procedures for cognitive training). Another task will be
L2215: watching a 20 minutes National Geographic documentary movie selected by the instructor at the beginning of
L2216: each session; at the end of the movie participants need to answer three questions about the watched movie.
L2217: These iPad tasks will be alternated between sessions to prevent boredom. The approximate time to perform both
L2218: tasks is 20 minutes which is the approximate time to complete the dual-task training on the iPad.
L2219: Active control sessions will be held in groups of up to 8 participants and will take place in the same context and
L2220: physical environment used for dual-task training sessions and lead by the same instructors. Studies compared
L2221: dual-task training to single-task training, as a control condition, in order to control for exposure of the same
L2222: specific tasks performed separately. [6] However, it can be argued that single- task training is not engaging or
L2223: stimulating enough and does not provide a feeling of progression. In response to this, it was recommended that
L2224: cognitive training studies use preferably sham trainings whereby clinician interaction and participant
L2225: expectation effects can be reliably matched. Therefore, this study will compare dual-task training to a nonspecific intervention, more comparable to cognitively stimulating activities that older adults can naturally
L2226: engage in. To assure same time exposure and same social interaction than in active intervention, the control
L2227: intervention will be held in groups using the same type of device that is used for the active intervention.
L2228: _**Details of Exercise intervention**_
L2229: The exercises training program involves 3 weekly sessions of approximately 60 min each. All sessions will be
L2230: held in appropriate gym facilities and take place between Monday and Friday ensuring that there are not three
L2231: consecutive days (i.e. training can occur on Monday, Wednesday and Friday or Monday, Thursday and Friday
L2232: etc.). Staff with experience and/or certification in exercises (graduate student in kinesiology, Canadian Society
L2233: of Exercise Physiology certificates, etc.) will supervise all training sessions with at least one coach per four
L2234: participants.
L2235: After a general warm-up, participants will execute the strength-training portion by performing light
L2236: strengthening exercises (pushes and pulls using elastic bands, chair stands, and forward lunges). The next phase
L2237: will include the following exercises: leg press, leg flexion (lower body), seated press, seated row, and seated
L2238: pull (upper body). These exercises are made in a circuit alternating between lower and upper body exercises. A
L2239: list of suggested exercises base on muscle groups will be provided to the trainers. The recovery period between
L2240: exercises corresponds to the time needed to move from one station to another. While the first portion of this
L2241: training protocol (12 weeks) will be dedicated to strength endurance exercises the following 12 weeks will
L2242: focus on maximal strength. Training volume and intensity are described in the table below. Sets represent the
L2243: number of circuit rounds while repetitions are maximal, which means that the resistance must be adjusted by the
L2244: trainer so that the participant reaches exhaustion at the last prescribed repetition of each set. Training
L2245: prescription for all exercises is made in accordance to the American College of Sport Medicine (ACSM)
L2246: guidelines for strength development in older adults (ACSM, version 1998).
L2247: Weeks Sets Repetitions Rest between sets (sec)
L2248: © 2023 Montero-Odasso M et al. _JAMA Network Open._
L2249: |Col1|Day 1|Day 2|Day 3|Day 1|Day 2|Day 3|Col8|
L2250: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
L2251: |1-4|2|2|2|15-18|15-18|15-18|30|
L2252: |5-8|3|2|3|12-15|12-15|12-15|30|
L2253: |9-12|3|2|3|10|10|10|60|
L2254: |13-16|2|3|2|8|12|8|60|
L2255: |17-20|3|3|4|6|8|6|60|
L2256: Following the strength portion of each session, an aerobic training will be prescribed. Participants will be asked
L2257: to complete 10-20 minutes of cardiovascular exercises using different ergometers (treadmill, elliptical
L2258: machines, cycling ergometers, rowing machines etc.), using steps or other forms of free aerobic exercises, as
L2259: long as the cognitive load is minimized. Intensity will be monitored using a Borg scale (0-10) and the target will
L2260: correspond to 5-6 for the first month, 6-7 for the second month and 7-8 for the last month. Details of the aerobic
L2261: training are presented in the table below. Each session will end with a five (5) minute recovery period, which
L2262: will consist of different breathing exercises and stretching.
L2263: |Weeks|Sets|Duration (min)|Intensity (Borg 0-10)|Rest between sets (minutes)|
L2264: |---|---|---|---|---|
L2265: |1-4|2|10|5-6|1|
L2266: |5-8|2|10|5-6|1|
L2267: |9-12|2|10|6-7|1|
L2268: |13-16|2|10|6-7|1|
L2269: |17-20|2|10|7-8|1|
L2270: Overall rate of perceived exertion (RPE) will be monitored using a Borg scale (0-10) 30 minutes after the end of
L2271: each session. Participants will have to rate the overall difficulty of the training session. These results will
L2272: provide information about the training load (session RPE – sRPE), monotony and strain.
L2273: _**Details of Control Exercise (Tone-Stretching Exercises)**_
L2274: Participants assigned in the control exercise condition will take part in tone-stretching sessions in groups of up
L2275: to 8 participants, supervised by a trainer. The exercises will be devoted to improve muscle tone, flexibility,
L2276: without improving, strength, cardiorespiratory capacity (and mobility). Resistant load and number of repetitions
L2277: will not progress across exercise sessions. The session will start with a 5-minute warm-up on the machine of
L2278: their choice. This will be followed by 50 minutes of stretching exercises that will target the entire body, one
L2279: joint at a time, from head to toes. Exercises will be performed in a seated position as much as possible. The
L2280: stretching program will be based on the one developed by Stanziano et al. [7] The session will end with 5 minutes
L2281: of relaxation. Variability in exercises performed between sessions will be encouraged in order to avoid
L2282: progression throughout the entire program.
L2283: _**Details of Vitamin D Supplementation**_
L2284: Participants will receive Vitamin D supplementation (1 tablet of 10,000IU of Vitamin D3) or matching placebo
L2285: three times per week in order to reach a weekly cumulative dose of 30,000 IU per week (equivalent of 4,258 IU
L2286: daily). The vitamin D capsules will be provided by the Research Coordinator (RC) in vials at baseline
L2287: assessment and every four weeks to complete 5 vials during the training period. Vials will be returned by the
L2288: participants to the RC at the end of each 4 weeks block. Rationale and bio- safety of the dose: The dose of
L2289: 10,000 IU/day is currently approved by Health Canada as a supplementation for elderly patients. Heaney et al.
L2290: have administered doses of 30,000 IU/day Vitamin D3 to adult men for five months, with no significant
L2291: changes in serum calcium concentrations or adverse reactions. [8-10] A comprehensive review of toxic effects of
L2292: vitamin D found that the lowest level at which an adverse effect was observed was a serum calcidiol
L2293: concentration of 200 nmol/l, corresponding to a daily intake of 40,000 IU. Therefore, three weekly doses of
L2294: 10,000IU have a 9-fold weekly margin of safety of the established safe dose.
L2295: © 2023 Montero-Odasso M et al. _JAMA Network Open._
L2296: _**Details of Placebo Vitamin D supplementation**_
L2297: Participants will receive Vitamin D supplementation (1 tablet of 10,000IU of Vitamin D3) or matching placebo
L2298: three times per week in order to reach a weekly cumulative dose of 30,000 IU per week (equivalent of 4,258 IU
L2299: daily). The vitamin D capsules will be provided by the Research Coordinator (RC) in vials at baseline
L2300: assessment and every four weeks to complete 5 vials during the training period. Vials will be returned by the
L2301: participants to the RC at the end of each 4 weeks block. Rationale and bio- safety of the dose: The dose of
L2302: 10,000 IU/day is currently approved by Health Canada as a supplementation for elderly patients. Heaney et al.
L2303: have administered doses of 30,000 IU/day Vitamin D3 to adult men for five months, with no significant
L2304: changes in serum calcium concentrations or adverse reactions. [8-10] A comprehensive review of toxic effects of
L2305: vitamin D found that the lowest level at which an adverse effect was observed was a serum calcidiol
L2306: concentration of 200 nmol/l, corresponding to a daily intake of 40,000 IU. Therefore, three weekly doses of
L2307: 10,000IU have a 9-fold weekly margin of safety of the established safe dose.
L2308: _**Supervision**_
L2309: All physical exercise training will be fully supervised by research assistants trained in exercise physiology or
L2310: with kinesiology or physical therapy backgrounds or training. Training frequency is 3 days/week. Each session
L2311: will take 90 minutes on average.
L2312: _**Additional tests included in the ADAS-Cog plus version**_
L2313: **1.** Trail-Making Test (TMT) A & B,
L2314: **2.** WAIS-R Digit Symbol Substitution Test (DSST)
L2315: **3.** Digit Span forward & backward (DSFB)
L2316: **4.** Category Fluency test (Animal and Vegetable)
